+# Adversarial review — crd-4a
+
+## High
+
+- None found in Pass 1.
+
+## Medium
+
+- **[Pass 1] Missing defender identity can satisfy attendance through `String(undefined) === String(undefined)`**
+  - **Severity:** Medium
+  - **File:line:** `server/routes/contested-rolls.js:145-166` (line numbers from the post-diff hunk)
+  - **Triggering input or sequence:** A power challenge has a missing `target_character_id`; the latest session has a malformed attendance entry whose `character_id` is also missing; game phase is active, a real challenger is found and attended, and the defender has higher City Status. `attendedIn(undefined, defenderChar.name)` considers the malformed attendance entry an ID match before needing a name match because both values stringify to `"undefined"`. The same issue exists for empty-string IDs. A missing/empty challenger ID normally closes the gate earlier because `new ObjectId(...)` cannot find the challenger (or throws), so the demonstrated accidental pass is on the defender side.
+  - **Observable consequence:** The at-Court gate can open and award a defensive bonus without evidence that the actual defender attended, provided both the challenge and attendance data are malformed in the matching way.
+  - **Confidence:** High in the comparison behavior; medium in production reachability because Pass 1 was not allowed to inspect earlier authorization/shape checks.
+
+- **[Pass 1] A truthy non-numeric Blood Potency can persist `NaN` as the defender pool**
+  - **Severity:** Medium
+  - **File:line:** `server/routes/contested-rolls.js:177-181,263-279`
+  - **Triggering input or sequence:** An eligible defender document has a truthy non-numeric `blood_potency` such as `"not-a-number"`, and the request strictly selects `defender_status_term: "bp"`. The returned `bp_value` remains that string; `pool + bp_value` concatenates, and the final `Math.min`/`Math.max` clamp coerces the result to `NaN`.
+  - **Observable consequence:** `defender_pool` is written as `NaN` and returned, violating the declared 0–30 numeric domain. The null-only guard does not reject non-finite totals.
+  - **Confidence:** High in JavaScript behavior and the shown write path; medium in reachability until the character schema is inspected in Pass 2.
+
+- **[Pass 1] The supposedly unchanged closed-gate path adds and returns `defender_status_term: null`**
+  - **Severity:** Medium
+  - **File:line:** `server/routes/contested-rolls.js:260-295`
+  - **Triggering input or sequence:** Any resolve for which `_statusChoiceEligibility` returns `null`, including an ordinary challenge with no `power_name`; whether or not the client sends a status term, `defenderStatusTerm` remains `null` and is unconditionally included in `$set`.
+  - **Observable consequence:** The submitted near-miss or valid term does not influence the pool and its value is not stored, because validation is strict `===`; however, the document is still mutated to contain `defender_status_term: null`, and the fetched response includes that field. Thus the non-gated persisted document and response shape are not unchanged as the comment claims.
+  - **Confidence:** High.
+
+## Low
+
+- **[Pass 1] `status_choice` is a response-only property that looks like part of the stored document**
+  - **Severity:** Low
+  - **File:line:** `server/routes/contested-rolls.js:292-295`
+  - **Triggering input or sequence:** Any eligible resolve performs `findOne`, then assigns `updated.status_choice = statusChoice` only on the in-memory Mongo result before serializing it.
+  - **Observable consequence:** The response presents `status_choice` beside persisted fields even though a later database read cannot retrieve it; future consumers can reasonably mistake the computed per-request projection for durable collection state. The complete diff contains no `$set` or other collection write for `status_choice`.
+  - **Confidence:** High.
+
+- **[Pass 1] Every truthy-power interactive resolve pays up to four fresh database reads**
+  - **Severity:** Low
+  - **File:line:** `server/routes/contested-rolls.js:127-177`
+  - **Triggering input or sequence:** A challenge has a nonblank string `power_name`. Each `/resolve` call (including repeated aspect, Willpower, merit, and status-term clicks) reads all chapters; when game phase is active it also reads the latest session and challenger; when attendance passes it reads all territories, even if City Status ultimately does not favor the defender.
+  - **Observable consequence:** Power-based resolution acquires repeated database latency and collection-scan work on an interactive endpoint. The comments explicitly justify fresh live evaluation and the initial `power_name` short-circuit, so this reads as a deliberate correctness tradeoff, but no caching/query consolidation or performance coverage is present in the diff.
+  - **Confidence:** High.
+
+- **[Pass 1] The client test claiming generation-guard reuse never creates an overlapping request**
+  - **Severity:** Low
+  - **File:line:** `server/tests/crd-3b-resolution-screen.test.js:535-561` (post-diff hunk)
+  - **Triggering input or sequence:** The test named “selecting a term ... reuses the existing generation-guard machinery” fully awaits the aspect resolve before clicking the status-term control, then fully awaits that resolve.
+  - **Observable consequence:** The test can pass even if stale response A overwrites newer response B, because it exercises only sequential calls. It does verify request wiring, pool rendering, and selection styling, but not the race behavior advertised by its title.
+  - **Confidence:** High.
+
+- **[Pass 1] New test scaffolding contains an unused import and unused accumulator**
+  - **Severity:** Low
+  - **File:line:** `server/tests/crd-4a-defensive-status-choice.test.js:25,36,51`
+  - **Triggering input or sequence:** Loading the new test file imports `beforeEach` and pushes inserted IDs into `seededCharIds`, but the diff never uses either afterward.
+  - **Observable consequence:** No runtime defect; this is dead test code introduced by the change and can mislead a maintainer into believing per-test setup or ID-based cleanup exists.
+  - **Confidence:** High.
+
+### Pass 1 freeze notes
+
+The missing eligible term does not produce an HTTP 400 on any shown branch: it produces HTTP 200 with `defender_pool: null` unless an unrelated earlier/outer failure occurs. Near-miss values (`"BP"`, `"bp "`, objects, arrays) fail the strict string comparisons and have no selected-term side effect. All newly interpolated character names and numeric display values shown in the client diff pass through the existing `esc(...)` convention. No other trivially satisfiable new assertion was identified.
+

